      "content": "<h3>3. Sorted collections: TreeSet, TreeMap, PriorityQueue</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Signature:</strong> <code>new TreeSet&lt;&gt;();  new TreeMap&lt;&gt;();  new PriorityQueue&lt;&gt;()</code></li>\n<li><strong>Returns:</strong> sorted containers</li>\n<li><strong>Time:</strong> O(log n) per add</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>For freshers:</strong> These containers keep elements ordered as you add them, using <code>compareTo</code>. A TreeSet of Comparable forts therefore prints in order and rejects duplicates.</p>\n<p><strong>For experienced developers:</strong> Here <code>compareTo</code> also decides <strong>duplicates</strong>: a TreeSet treats two objects as the same when <code>compareTo</code> returns 0, even if <code>equals</code> says they differ.</p>",
      "codeExamples": [
        "// TreeSet and TreeMap order a Comparable class automatically\nimport java.util.TreeMap;\nimport java.util.TreeSet;\n\npublic class ComparableCollectionsDemo {\n    record Fort(String name) implements Comparable<Fort> {\n        public int compareTo(Fort other) {\n            return name.compareTo(other.name);\n        }\n    }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        TreeSet<Fort> forts = new TreeSet<>();\n        forts.add(new Fort(\"Torna\"));\n        forts.add(new Fort(\"Raigad\"));\n        forts.add(new Fort(\"Raigad\"));                               // compareTo == 0: a duplicate\n        System.out.println(\"TreeSet : \" + forts);\n\n        TreeMap<Fort, Integer> hours = new TreeMap<>();\n        hours.put(new Fort(\"Torna\"), 5);\n        hours.put(new Fort(\"Lohagad\"), 2);\n        System.out.println(\"TreeMap : \" + hours);\n    }\n}\n// OUTPUT:\n// TreeSet : [Fort[name=Raigad], Fort[name=Torna]]\n// TreeMap : {Fort[name=Lohagad]=2, Fort[name=Torna]=5}"
      ]
