      "content": "<h3>11. TreeSet and TreeMap (always sorted)</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Signature:</strong> <code>new TreeSet&lt;&gt;();  new TreeMap&lt;&gt;()</code></li>\n<li><strong>Returns:</strong> a container kept sorted</li>\n<li><strong>Time:</strong> O(log n) per add / put</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>For freshers:</strong> You do not call sort at all: every element goes into its correct place when added, and printing shows the order.</p>\n<p><strong>For experienced developers:</strong> Backed by a red-black tree. <code>compareTo == 0</code> means \"same element\", so equal-ordered objects are merged.</p>",
      "codeExamples": [
        "// TreeMap keeps its keys in compareTo order\nimport java.util.TreeMap;\n\npublic class CmpTreeMapDemo {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        TreeMap<String, Integer> trekHours = new TreeMap<>();\n        trekHours.put(\"Torna\", 5);\n        trekHours.put(\"Raigad\", 6);\n        trekHours.put(\"Lohagad\", 2);\n        System.out.println(trekHours);                            // sorted by key\n    }\n}\n// OUTPUT:\n// {Lohagad=2, Raigad=6, Torna=5}"
      ]
